You are working on the ExamFlow project.

We are now starting:

PHASE 6 — CROWDMARK EXAM TEMPLATE IMPORT & INTEGRATION

The goal of this phase is to integrate ExamFlow with the existing Crowdmark
workflow without attempting to replace or replicate Crowdmark.

ExamFlow is responsible for:

- exam administration
- student roster
- room assignment
- seat assignment
- exam metadata
- document preparation metadata

Crowdmark remains responsible for:

- exam templates
- exam questions
- digital/scanned exam workflows
- grading
- Crowdmark-specific exam management

IMPORTANT:

Keep the integration intentionally simple.

The preferred architecture is:

Crowdmark
   ↓
Export exam template
   ↓
ExamFlow
   ↓
Import and store template metadata/file
   ↓
Associate template with Exam
   ↓
Later generate personalized exam documents

Do NOT attempt to recreate Crowdmark.

Do NOT implement a full Crowdmark clone.

Do NOT build grading functionality.

Do NOT build an OCR system.

Do NOT implement AI.

Do NOT generate personalized exam PDFs yet.

Do NOT generate QR codes yet.

Those belong to later phases.

==================================================
1. READ PROJECT DOCUMENTATION
==================================================

Before coding, read:

- PRD.md
- ARCHITECTURE.md
- DATABASE.md
- SECURITY.md
- DEVELOPMENT.md
- AGENTS.md

Also inspect:

- Phase 1 Authentication
- Phase 2 Exam Management
- Phase 3 Student/Roster Management
- Phase 4 Room/Seat Management
- Phase 5 Seating Assignment

Follow the existing architecture.

Do not introduce a new architecture.

Before coding, provide a concise implementation plan.

==================================================
2. CORE DESIGN PRINCIPLE
==================================================

Crowdmark and ExamFlow have different responsibilities.

Crowdmark:

"How is the exam administered and graded?"

ExamFlow:

"Who is writing the exam, where are they sitting, and what document should
be prepared for them?"

Do not duplicate Crowdmark functionality.

==================================================
3. INTEGRATION STRATEGY
==================================================

Use an import-first architecture.

Initial workflow:

1. Instructor/administrator creates an exam in ExamFlow.
2. User exports/downloads the exam template from Crowdmark.
3. User uploads the exported template into ExamFlow.
4. ExamFlow validates the file.
5. ExamFlow stores the template securely.
6. ExamFlow associates the template with the Exam.
7. ExamFlow records Crowdmark metadata if available.
8. Later phases use the template to generate personalized exam documents.

Do not require a live Crowdmark API for the first implementation.

==================================================
4. NO CROWDMARK API DEPENDENCY
==================================================

Do NOT make the system dependent on a Crowdmark API unless a documented,
supported API is explicitly available and required.

The first version must work with:

Crowdmark export/download
→
ExamFlow upload

This keeps the system:

- simple
- maintainable
- independent
- easier for central Registrar deployment

If future API integration is appropriate, design the code so it can be added
later.

==================================================
5. EXAM TEMPLATE MODEL
==================================================

Create an ExamTemplate entity.

Suggested fields:

id
exam_id
original_filename
stored_filename
mime_type
file_size
file_hash
template_type
version
status
created_by
created_at
updated_at

Use the project's existing ID strategy.

Do not create unnecessary fields.

==================================================
6. TEMPLATE RELATIONSHIP

An Exam can have one or more template versions.

Recommended:

Exam
  ↓
ExamTemplate
  ├── version 1
  ├── version 2
  └── version 3

Only one template should be marked as the active template.

Database/business logic must prevent ambiguous active templates.

==================================================
7. TEMPLATE STATUS

Keep status simple.

Possible:

ACTIVE
ARCHIVED

If the architecture does not need multiple states, use:

is_active

Do not create a large workflow state machine.

==================================================
8. TEMPLATE VERSION

Support basic versioning.

Example:

Exam:
CSC108 Final

Templates:

v1 — original
v2 — corrected
v3 — final

The administrator can select which version is active.

Do not implement Git-like version control.

==================================================
9. FILE TYPES

The system should support the file formats actually exported by the current
Crowdmark workflow.

Before implementing format-specific parsing:

inspect the actual exported format available to the project.

Do NOT assume that Crowdmark exports are always PDF.

The system should identify:

- PDF
- image files
- archive files
- other supported document formats

only if they are actually part of the approved workflow.

If only PDF is required by the PRD, support PDF only.

Do not build unnecessary parsers.

==================================================
10. FILE VALIDATION

Uploaded files must be validated.

Validate:

- extension
- MIME type
- file size
- file signature / magic bytes where appropriate
- file integrity

Do not trust the filename extension.

Example:

A file called:

exam.pdf

must not automatically be trusted as a valid PDF.

==================================================
11. FILE SIZE

Define a reasonable maximum upload size.

Use application configuration.

Do not hard-code arbitrary values throughout the code.

Example configuration:

MAX_EXAM_TEMPLATE_SIZE

The exact production value should be configurable.

==================================================
12. SECURE STORAGE

Do NOT store uploaded exam files directly in the public frontend directory.

Do NOT expose uploaded files through:

/public
/static
/public/uploads

unless there is a strong security reason and proper access control.

Exam templates may contain confidential exam material.

Store them in protected storage.

For the initial implementation, local filesystem storage is acceptable if
consistent with the architecture.

Design the storage interface so it can later support:

- object storage
- Azure Blob Storage
- S3-compatible storage

without changing business logic.

==================================================
13. STORAGE ABSTRACTION

Create a simple storage service.

Conceptually:

ExamTemplateStorage

Methods may include:

save()
get()
delete()
exists()

Do not create a large generic storage framework.

The service should isolate file storage from database logic.

==================================================
14. FILE NAMING

Do not store files using the original filename directly.

Do not allow:

../../../etc/passwd

or similar path manipulation.

Generate safe internal filenames.

Example concept:

exam-template-{UUID}.pdf

The original filename should be stored as metadata only.

==================================================
15. PATH TRAVERSAL

Never construct storage paths directly from user-provided filenames.

Normalize and sanitize all file-related inputs.

Do not trust:

filename
path
directory
extension

from the client.

==================================================
16. FILE HASH

Calculate a cryptographic hash for uploaded files.

Recommended:

SHA-256

Store:

file_hash

This provides:

- duplicate detection
- integrity verification
- auditability

Do not use MD5 as the primary integrity identifier.

==================================================
17. DUPLICATE FILE

If the same file is uploaded twice:

detect it using:

exam_id + file_hash

The UI should clearly indicate:

"This template already exists."

Do not silently create duplicate versions.

==================================================
18. TEMPLATE METADATA

Store useful metadata:

Original filename
File type
File size
SHA-256
Version
Uploaded by
Uploaded time

Do not store unnecessary file metadata.

==================================================
19. CROWDMARK IDENTIFIER

If the exported template or workflow provides a Crowdmark exam identifier,
store it.

Possible field:

crowdmark_exam_id

However:

Do not assume a specific Crowdmark identifier format.

Treat it as an opaque string.

Do not validate it using guessed formats.

==================================================
20. CROWDMark URL

If the user provides a Crowdmark exam URL and the product requirements
support storing it:

store it as metadata.

Do not automatically fetch arbitrary URLs.

Do not use the URL as a security credential.

Do not expose it to unauthorized users.

==================================================
21. NO CROWDMark PASSWORDS

Never store:

Crowdmark password
Crowdmark session cookie
Crowdmark authentication token

in the database.

Never ask users to paste Crowdmark passwords into ExamFlow.

==================================================
22. OPTIONAL CROWDMARK METADATA

If useful, support:

crowdmark_exam_id
crowdmark_url

Keep these fields optional.

ExamFlow must remain functional if Crowdmark metadata is not available.

==================================================
23. TEMPLATE UPLOAD API

Implement an endpoint such as:

POST
/api/exams/{exam_id}/templates

The endpoint must:

- authenticate user
- authorize access to exam
- validate exam
- validate uploaded file
- calculate hash
- store file
- create database record
- return metadata

==================================================
24. TEMPLATE LIST API

Implement:

GET
/api/exams/{exam_id}/templates

Return:

id
original filename
version
file type
file size
hash
status
uploaded by
created_at

Do not return file contents.

==================================================
25. TEMPLATE DOWNLOAD API

Implement a protected endpoint:

GET
/api/exam-templates/{template_id}/download

The endpoint must:

- authenticate
- authorize
- locate template
- stream the file
- use a safe content disposition

Do not expose the filesystem path.

==================================================
26. TEMPLATE DELETE

Do NOT permanently delete active templates.

If deletion is required:

archive them.

Historical exam documents may depend on template versions.

Prefer:

ARCHIVED

over destructive deletion.

==================================================
27. TEMPLATE ACTIVATE

Implement:

POST
/api/exam-templates/{template_id}/activate

Only authorized users may activate a template.

Activating a template should:

- deactivate previous active template
- activate selected template
- execute atomically

==================================================
28. TEMPLATE VERSION CREATION

When uploading a new valid template:

If the file differs from the active template:

create a new version.

Example:

Current:

v1 ACTIVE

Upload new file:

v2 ACTIVE

Previous:

v1 ARCHIVED

Do not overwrite the original file.

==================================================
29. VERSION NUMBER

Version numbering should be generated server-side.

Do not trust:

version = 999

from the frontend.

The server determines the next version.

==================================================
30. EXAM TEMPLATE UI

On:

/app/exams/[id]

add:

Exam Template

Display:

Current template:
CSC108-Final.pdf
Version: 3
Status: Active

Buttons:

[ Upload Template ]
[ Download ]
[ View Versions ]

==================================================
31. UPLOAD UI

Provide:

Choose File

Optional:

Crowdmark Exam ID
Crowdmark URL

Then:

[ Upload ]

Show:

- file name
- size
- upload progress if supported
- validation errors
- success state

==================================================
32. TEMPLATE VERSION UI

Display:

Version | File | Size | Uploaded By | Date | Status

Example:

v3 | final.pdf | 4.2 MB | admin | Aug 19 | Active
v2 | revised.pdf | 4.1 MB | admin | Aug 18 | Archived
v1 | original.pdf | 4.0 MB | admin | Aug 17 | Archived

Actions:

Download
Activate

Do not show unnecessary technical metadata to normal users.

==================================================
33. TEMPLATE SECURITY

Exam templates are confidential.

Require authentication.

Require authorization.

Do not allow:

anonymous download
public URLs
predictable file paths

==================================================
34. DOWNLOAD AUTHORIZATION

A user who can access Exam A must not automatically be able to download:

Exam B's template.

Check:

user authorization
→
exam authorization
→
template relationship

Do not authorize solely based on template ID.

==================================================
35. IDOR PROTECTION

Test:

Change:

/api/exam-templates/{template_id}

to another valid UUID.

The unauthorized user must receive:

403 or 404

according to the application's security convention.

==================================================
36. CONTENT SECURITY

Do not render uploaded exam files directly as HTML.

If displaying PDFs inline:

use an appropriate content type and security headers.

Do not execute uploaded content.

==================================================
37. MALICIOUS FILES

Uploaded files are untrusted.

Do not:

execute them
import them as code
treat them as configuration
parse arbitrary archive contents without validation

If archive formats are supported later, implement strict extraction rules.

For Phase 6, prefer supporting the simplest safe file type required.

==================================================
38. PDF VALIDATION

If PDF is supported:

validate that the file is actually a PDF.

Do not rely solely on:

filename.endsWith(".pdf")

Use file signature and a safe parser where appropriate.

==================================================
39. TEMPLATE PAGE COUNT

If PDF parsing is already available and safe:

extract:

page_count

This can be useful metadata.

However:

do not introduce a heavyweight PDF processing system just to obtain page
count.

If not necessary, defer it.

==================================================
40. TEMPLATE CONTENT PARSING

Do NOT attempt to parse exam questions.

Do NOT attempt to understand:

- questions
- answers
- grading rubrics
- student responses

Crowdmark remains responsible for exam content.

ExamFlow only needs the template as a document asset.

==================================================
41. TEMPLATE IDENTITY

The system should treat:

file_hash

as the identity of the uploaded file contents.

Do not use:

filename

as the unique identity.

Two files may have the same filename but different content.

==================================================
42. IMPORT WORKFLOW

The user workflow should be:

Step 1

Create Exam in ExamFlow.

Step 2

Create exam in Crowdmark.

Step 3

Export/download Crowdmark exam template.

Step 4

Return to ExamFlow.

Step 5

Upload template.

Step 6

Optionally enter Crowdmark exam ID.

Step 7

ExamFlow validates and stores the template.

Step 8

Template becomes available for later personalization.

==================================================
43. DO NOT AUTO-FETCH

Do not implement:

"Enter Crowdmark URL and automatically download exam."

unless an approved official API/integration is available and explicitly
required.

Manual upload is preferred for the first production version.

==================================================
44. IMPORT SUMMARY

After upload, display:

Exam:
CSC108 Final

Template:
CSC108-final.pdf

Version:
3

Pages:
10

Size:
4.2 MB

Hash:
xxxxxxxx

Status:
Active

Crowdmark Exam ID:
optional

==================================================
45. TEMPLATE ↔ EXAM RELATIONSHIP

The template belongs to the Exam.

Do not create a global template library in Phase 6.

Avoid:

Template
  ↓
multiple unrelated exams

unless PRD.md explicitly requires reusable templates.

Keep:

Exam
  ↓
ExamTemplate

simple.

==================================================
46. FUTURE PERSONALIZATION

Phase 7 will use:

ExamTemplate
+
Student
+
SeatAssignment

to generate:

Personalized Exam

Example:

Template:
CSC108-final.pdf

Student:
100000001 Alice Student

Room:
IB101

Seat:
A01

Output:

Alice's personalized exam document

Do NOT implement this in Phase 6.

==================================================
47. FUTURE QR CODE

Phase 7/8 may add a QR code linking:

personalized exam
→
Crowdmark identity

Do NOT generate QR codes in Phase 6.

The database model should not depend on QR codes yet.

==================================================
48. FUTURE CROWDMark SUBMISSION

Do not implement:

upload answers to Crowdmark
submit exams to Crowdmark
retrieve grades
sync grading status

Those are separate future capabilities.

==================================================
49. CROWDMark INTEGRATION ABSTRACTION

If an integration service is created, keep it small.

For example:

CrowdmarkIntegrationService

Possible future methods:

get_exam()
upload_template()
create_submission()

But Phase 6 should implement only what is actually needed.

Do not create empty fake API implementations.

==================================================
50. EXTERNAL API

If a Crowdmark API is not required or officially available:

do not create an HTTP client.

The system should still be fully functional.

==================================================
51. CONFIGURATION

Do not add Crowdmark secrets to the project.

If future configuration is needed:

CROWDMARK_ENABLED
CROWDMARK_BASE_URL

Only if actually required.

Do not create fake credentials.

==================================================
52. DATABASE MIGRATION

Create migration for:

exam_templates

Include:

- primary key
- exam foreign key
- creator foreign key
- file hash
- version
- status
- timestamps

Add appropriate indexes.

==================================================
53. DATABASE CONSTRAINTS

Enforce:

exam_id + version unique

If only one active template is allowed:

enforce this at the application/database level appropriately.

Do not allow duplicate versions.

==================================================
54. FILE STORAGE TRANSACTION SAFETY

Be careful with:

database transaction
vs
filesystem storage

A database transaction cannot automatically roll back a filesystem write.

Implement safe cleanup.

Example:

1. validate file
2. calculate hash
3. save temporary file
4. create database record
5. finalize file
6. cleanup temporary file if database operation fails

Do not leave orphan files.

==================================================
55. UPLOAD TEMPORARY FILES

Use a temporary location for uploads.

Do not directly place unvalidated uploads into permanent storage.

Workflow:

upload
→ validate
→ hash
→ temporary storage
→ database transaction
→ final storage

==================================================
56. FILE ACCESS

Use streaming for downloads.

Do not load large exam templates entirely into application memory.

==================================================
57. AUDIT EVENTS

Create audit events:

EXAM_TEMPLATE_UPLOADED
EXAM_TEMPLATE_ACTIVATED
EXAM_TEMPLATE_ARCHIVED
EXAM_TEMPLATE_DOWNLOADED

Audit should contain:

actor
exam_id
template_id
action
timestamp
safe summary

Do not log file contents.

Do not log student PII.

==================================================
58. SECURITY LOGGING

Do not log:

- exam PDF contents
- Crowdmark credentials
- tokens
- passwords
- full file paths if they expose internal storage
- unnecessary sensitive metadata

==================================================
59. TESTING — UPLOAD

Test:

1. valid file
2. invalid extension
3. invalid MIME type
4. oversized file
5. corrupted PDF
6. duplicate hash
7. unauthorized upload
8. missing exam
9. safe filename handling

==================================================
60. TESTING — VERSIONING

Test:

1. upload v1
2. upload different file
3. verify v2
4. verify v1 archived
5. activate v1
6. verify v2 archived
7. activate v2
8. verify only one active template

==================================================
61. TESTING — DOWNLOAD

Test:

- authorized download
- unauthorized download
- nonexistent template
- correct content type
- correct filename
- no filesystem path exposure

==================================================
62. TESTING — IDOR

Explicitly test:

User A has access to Exam A.

User A attempts to download:

Exam B template.

Must fail.

==================================================
63. TESTING — STORAGE FAILURE

Simulate:

filesystem/object storage failure

Verify:

database does not contain a broken template record.

==================================================
64. TESTING — DATABASE FAILURE

Simulate:

database failure after temporary file creation.

Verify:

temporary file is cleaned up.

No orphan file should remain.

==================================================
65. TESTING — CONCURRENCY

Test two uploads occurring at approximately the same time.

Verify:

version numbers remain unique and consistent.

==================================================
66. FRONTEND TESTING

Test:

- upload form
- validation errors
- upload success
- version list
- active template
- archive state
- download
- activation
- unauthorized behavior

==================================================
67. ACCESSIBILITY

Ensure:

- keyboard-accessible upload
- clear labels
- accessible errors
- accessible confirmation dialogs
- semantic tables
- visible focus states

==================================================
68. PERFORMANCE

Do not unnecessarily process exam documents.

For Phase 6:

The system primarily:

- validates
- stores
- retrieves metadata
- streams files

Do not render PDF pages or perform expensive document processing unless
required.

==================================================
69. STORAGE CONFIGURATION

Support configurable storage.

Example:

STORAGE_BACKEND=local

Future:

STORAGE_BACKEND=s3
STORAGE_BACKEND=azure

Do not implement all storage backends now.

A simple interface is sufficient.

==================================================
70. LOCAL STORAGE

For development:

use a configurable application storage directory.

Do not use:

/var/www/html
/public
/static

as the default upload location.

The path must be outside publicly served frontend assets.

==================================================
71. PRODUCTION SAFETY

Do not commit uploaded templates.

Ensure:

.gitignore

contains the local storage directory.

Never commit actual exam material.

==================================================
72. PRIVACY

Exam templates may contain confidential examination material.

Treat them as restricted institutional data.

Do not expose them through:

- public URLs
- CDN without authorization
- frontend static directories
- logs
- analytics

